From 0b7277a601f6ca310ca4ee12b17c0b65e099cfef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 07:44:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 20/23] feat(logs): classify failures so a recorded reason is
 never empty
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`str(exc)` is an empty string for every httpx timeout, a bare ConnectError
and asyncio.TimeoutError — and every failure path stored exactly that. The
job row, the RequestLog, the ledger event and the UI all ended up with
nothing, which is why a failed video reached the user as "Video generation
failed" and left the operator with `Video job <id> failed:` in the container
log.

`describe_failure()` returns a stable code (timeout / connect_error /
network_error / http_client_error / http_server_error / invalid_response /
provider_error / cancelled), a message that always says something — the
exception class when there is no text, plus the HTTP status, the request's
method and host, and a slice of the error body — and the upstream status
code for the column added next. URLs are quoted without their query string:
video assets and object storage arrive as signed links.

Wired into the video worker, the chat error formatter (which handed the
client a blank SSE error frame on a timeout), transcription, chat titles and
image prompts. Two video-specific holes closed with it: a provider that
reports a terminal state with no reason produced `RuntimeError(None)` —
literally "None" in the log — and now names the state and quotes its last
payload; and the OpenRouter adapter now reads a structured `error` object
instead of stringifying the dict or dropping it.

